A||||12||Dick McHaffie||Is this a fuel filter problem?||||5||
Z||000000||Dick McHaffie||03-01-2000||12:36 AM||||Last night at 10:30pm, cold Oregon rainstorm. I have to go gas up the Fiero for work.  My kids been driving it all weekend but I thought it had enough gas to make it to Rice Hill...The 85GT dies about 3/4mile from the station, so I walk thru the rain, get a can of gas, walk back up and put the gas in.  But it won't start!<P>I'm thinking fuel pump or filter..<P>I call the wife, she and our daughter come out and we push it to the station...it won't even push start!!! and we are rolling down a pretty good hill.<P>So I let it sit overnight at the station, and go back tonight when I get off work.  It starts right up like theres nothing wrong.  I drive it home without a hitch.<P>Later my daughter wants to take the fiero to her boyfriends house...Won't Start!<P>What do you think guys? Fuel filter?  If so I sure hope me its an easy fix and not something I have to take the tank off to accomplish.<P>Thanks, you guys are the best!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||03-01-2000||02:31 AM||||I hesitate to answer cause I have no idea of what the fix is.  But it does not sound like a fuel filter.  The on again off again nature points towards an electrical problem rather than mechanical.<BR>Oregon, I bet if you took the donut spare apart there would be green stuff growing on the inside.  America's own rain forest.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Dick McHaffie||03-01-2000||04:47 PM||||Yep, its pretty damp here from October to May, but I tell you there is no better place to be in the summertime than here in the Umpqua Valley.  I warms up to 80-85 on average, rarely over 90 and NOOOOOO humidity.<P>Actually the winters aren't bad.  Just wet.<P>So you think its electrical... I know my dad had a problem with a truck of his years ago that had a piece of tree leaf in the gas that would block the fuel line from time to time.  In between, it ran perfectly. (It drove him absolutely nuts!)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||03-01-2000||06:36 PM||||I actually had a similar problem with my old '85....  I gave it a good tune-up including a fuel filter, cap, rotor, plugs, wires etc. and the problem vanished.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-01-2000||07:58 PM||||could be a fuel pump problem. I doubt a filter.<P>I'd check ignition first. disconect the tach filter. that stuff first. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||bad88||03-02-2000||01:45 AM||||when was the last time the filter was replaced?  If you aren't sure it can't hurt to replace it.  Try some gas boost in the tank too to help remove any moisture that might be in there.  I would take this route first since it's not too expensive and it can't hurt more than it helps.  As far as electrical....it could be.  Do the guages drop before the car dies?  That would be a good indication of electrical problem causing the shutdown.  You might want to check the spark also, make sure the wires are in good shape and the cap isn't cracked||||reg||3
Z||000006||William Federle||03-02-2000||07:07 AM||||I've never had this problem but I've read about similar things more than once on this forum. It might not be coincidental that running out of gas was followed by this problem. When you ran out of gas, the last gas you got from the tank might have been very dirty gas. Also, your fuel pump had an opportunity to run dry which might have caused bits of debris to be put in your tank and sucked up later. The fuel filter guess might not be far off, but a new one may not fix things if there is still crud in the fuel line.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dick McHaffie||03-03-2000||01:32 AM||||Well, it sounds like its fuel related anyway.<P>The thing started when i got home from work, my daughter drove it to a friends house and back, went out an hour or so later and it wouldn't start.....<P>I guess maybe I should catch it when it won't start, disconnect the fuel line, and turn the engine to see if gas is getting through.  Or is that a bad idea?<P>I have tomorrow off & I was hoping I could tackle this.||||reg||1
Z||000008||b_b_a_d||03-03-2000||04:09 AM||||IGNITION COIL, IGNITION COIL, IGNITION COIL...<P>In my ever so humble opinion,<P>I am thinking it is time to replace the ignition coil.  I'm not sure, but I believe they can be tested at a reliable auto parts store.  I know they can check the ignition module.  <P>Be forwarned about parts stores checking out your stuff though as I had a young inexperienced kid (no offense to anyone) take mine in the back and return telling me it was fine. (He was wrong) <P>Usually when this goes out, it is pretty slim your car will start again by turning the key.<P>You can push start a car on some occasions with a bad coil, but not always.<P><BR>As far as Fuel is concerned, an old backyard mechanics trick is to take off the air intake going into the intake manifold and take a little bottle (plant water spray bottles work best) and fill it with a little gasoline.  The trick here is to spray a little gas into the intake while somebody turns the key.  If it fires up then you pretty much isolated the problem to lack of fuel one way or another.<P>Don't be afraid to spray a few times as the engine is turning over.  You can actually keep the car running (rough mind you) but it does work.  Just be careful and use common sense.  I don't want to hear about how somebody's fiero burned to the ground.      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>If you can get the car running by spraying the gas into the intake then chances are it is the fuel pump.<P>Best of luck, but I still think it is the ignition coil.       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by b_b_a_d (edited 03-03-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000009||Dick McHaffie||03-04-2000||09:19 PM||||Thanks to everyone for all the help.<P>The problem appears to have been corroded pins on my ignition module.<P>I tried spraying gas into the manifold when it was in one of its "wont start" moods, that did nothing.<P>Pulled a plug, it was getting no spark.<P>Pulled the coil and had it tested by a mechanic friend, it was OK.  He said it might be the ignition module, so I popped it out and the pins were so bad, I can't believe there was ever a connection made.<P>I cleaned them off, put 'er back together and boom! Back in the saddle again!.<P>Thanks again to everyone||||reg||6
Z||000010||William Federle||03-04-2000||09:29 PM||||Thanks for the info on what the real cause turned out to be.||||reg||14
Z||000011||b_b_a_d||03-05-2000||04:16 AM||||Yes...  Thank you for the insight as to the actual problem!!!  I document these sort of things just for future personal reference.<P>Congradulations!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Always happy to see a car back on the road!||||reg||14
Z||000012||Trixi||03-05-2000||10:05 AM||||Try tightening the starter wires.  84's tend to die out and not start, so since yours is only an 85, try it and see.||||reg||3
